'''''Helianthemum apenninum''''', the '''white rock-rose''', is a white-flowering [[Helianthemum|rock rose]] of the family [[Cistaceae]] found in the North Atlantic region, mainly in dry grassy and rocky places across large parts of Europe.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Helianthemum apenninum (L.) Mill. {{!}} Plants of the World Online {{!}} Kew Science |url=http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:168741-1 |access-date=2022-03-08 |website=Plants of the World Online |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=White Rock-rose - Encyclopedia of Life |url=https://eol.org/pages/5732367 |access-date=2022-03-08 |website=eol.org}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Helianthemum apenninum : White Rock-rose {{!}} NBN Atlas |url=https://species.nbnatlas.org/species/NHMSYS0000459259#overview |access-date=2022-03-08 |website=species.nbnatlas.org}}</ref>

''Helianthemum apenninum'', is a self supporting, semi-woody shrub that may grow up to {{Convert|0.5|m|sp=us}} high and roots {{Convert|0.3|m|sp=us}} deep. It follows a perennial life cycle.<ref name=":0" />

It flowers from March to July and produces pentamerous flowers, white with yellow centers and yellow [[stamen]]s and up to 30&nbsp;mm across. The three outer sepals are hairy and striped; the 2 inner sepals are very small. The flowers grow in clusters of 3 to 10 on a stem.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Helianthemum apenninum : White Rock-rose {{!}} NBN Atlas |url=https://species.nbnatlas.org/species/NHMSYS0000459259 |access-date=2022-03-08 |website=species.nbnatlas.org}}</ref> The flowers are visited by [[Micropterix calthella|Marsh Marygold Moth]] (''Micropterix calthella'').<ref name=":0" />

It produces [[Dried fruit|Dry fruits]] with seeds (average 0.9&nbsp;mg) that are dispersed through [[Seed dispersal|autochory]] (un-assisted or gravity-assisted dispersion).<ref name=":0" />

It is photoautotroph and an [[Evergreen|Evergreen Plant]] (maintains foliage throughout the year). The leaves are small (around 30&nbsp;mm), linear/lance-shaped, in opposite pairs. They are covered with greyish or whitish hairs, especially on the underside, and the edges are rolled under.<ref name=":1" />

